Section 1.04: Definition of Material Adverse Effect  
The term "Material Adverse Effect" means any event, change, circumstance, development, or occurrence that, individually or in the aggregate, has had or would reasonably be expected to have a material adverse effect on the business, assets, liabilities, financial condition, results of operations, or prospects of the Company and its Subsidiaries, taken as a whole; provided, however, that no event, change, circumstance, development, or occurrence shall be taken into account in determining whether a Material Adverse Effect has occurred or would reasonably be expected to occur to the extent that such event, change, circumstance, development, or occurrence arises out of or results from:  
(a) changes in general economic, political, or financial market conditions (including changes in interest rates, credit markets, or currency exchange rates) in the United States or elsewhere, or changes in general business, economic, or financial conditions in the industries in which the Company and its Subsidiaries operate, in each case, to the extent such changes are not disproportionately adverse to the Company and its Subsidiaries relative to other participants in such industries;  
(b) changes in laws, regulations, or accounting standards, or the interpretation or enforcement thereof, in the United States or elsewhere, to the extent such changes are not disproportionately adverse to the Company and its Subsidiaries relative to other participants in such industries;  
(c) any action taken or omitted to be taken by the Company or any of its Subsidiaries with the prior written consent of the Buyer or as required by applicable law or this Agreement;  
(d) any failure by the Company or any of its Subsidiaries to meet any internal or published projections, forecasts, or revenue or earnings predictions (it being understood that the underlying causes of such failure may be taken into account to the extent otherwise permitted under this definition);  
(e) any outbreak or escalation of hostilities, war, acts of terrorism, or any natural disaster, pandemic, or other force majeure event, to the extent such event is not disproportionately adverse to the Company and its Subsidiaries relative to other participants in such industries;  
(f) any change in the trading price or volume of the Company’s common stock or any other securities of the Company, or any failure to meet any internal or published projections, forecasts, or revenue or earnings predictions, in each case, solely as a result of such change in price or volume or failure to meet such projections;  
(g) any change in the Company’s or any of its Subsidiaries’ credit ratings or creditworthiness, or any change in the Company’s or any of its Subsidiaries’ ability to obtain financing, in each case, solely as a result of such change in credit ratings or creditworthiness or ability to obtain financing;  
(h) any action taken or omitted to be taken by the Buyer or any of its Affiliates, or any action taken or omitted to be taken by the Company or any of its Subsidiaries with the prior written consent of the Buyer;  
(i) any change in the Company’s or any of its Subsidiaries’ ability to obtain or maintain any licenses, permits, or authorizations, or any change in the scope or terms of any such licenses, permits, or authorizations, to the extent such change is not disproportionately adverse to the Company and its Subsidiaries relative to other participants in such industries;  
(j) any change in the Company’s or any of its Subsidiaries’ ability to retain or hire key employees, or any change in the Company’s or any of its Subsidiaries’ ability to maintain relationships with customers, suppliers, or other third parties, to the extent such change is not disproportionately adverse to the Company and its Subsidiaries relative to other participants in such industries;  
(k) any change in the Company’s or any of its Subsidiaries’ ability to maintain or enforce intellectual property rights, or any change in the scope or terms of any such rights, to the extent such change is not disproportionately adverse to the Company and its Subsidiaries relative to other participants in such industries;  
(l) any change in the Company’s or any of its Subsidiaries’ ability to maintain or enforce contractual rights, or any change in the scope or terms of any such rights, to the extent such change is not disproportionately adverse to the Company and its Subsidiaries relative to other participants in such industries;  
(m) any change in the Company’s or any of its Subsidiaries’ ability to maintain or enforce any legal or regulatory compliance obligations, or any change in the scope or terms of any such obligations, to the extent such change is not disproportionately adverse to the Company and its Subsidiaries relative to other participants in such industries;  
(n) any change in the Company’s or any of its Subsidiaries’ ability to maintain or enforce any environmental, health, or safety compliance obligations
